WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.680
Narrator: Here is a shot from
"Clifford the Big Red Dog"

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:05.904
before the beloved pup was added with CG.

00:00:05.904 --> 00:00:07.064
[Clifford sniffing]

00:00:07.064 --> 00:00:08.780
[girl screams]

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.320
To get the proper reference footage

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.980
and give the cast something to react to,

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.130
a 10-foot-high practical
version of Clifford was used,

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:18.680
and two puppeteers had to
carry the massive puppy

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.000
through New York City.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.130
That's just one of many
movies that came out in 2021

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:27.700
that involved risky car
stunts, innovative camerawork,

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.967
and a few creature performers.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.625
After many previous
out-of-this-world stunts,

00:00:39.463 --> 00:00:41.640
the latest "Fast &amp; Furious" installment

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.298
took it to another level
with magnetized cars.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.730
The team had to create
a magnetization effect

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.910
with very little CGI.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.950
Stunt coordinator Andy
Gill used customized cars

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.940
with hidden ramps, locking mechanisms,

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.610
and cables to give the
appearance of magnets

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.713
pulling and releasing other vehicles.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.340
And for this scene, when the cars speed by

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.070
store-lined city streets,

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:12.233
appliances actually flew
through the store windows.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.050
Each appliance had an attached tube

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.270
filled with compressed air.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:19.380
When carefully cued,

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.400
they were launched at the cars one by one

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.291
while whizzing by
strategically placed extras.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.420
Whether it was dancing on the wall,

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.420
a huge pool musical number,

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.660
or making these heads bob
practically in the hair salon,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.560
filmmakers found innovative ways

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.077
to bring the award-winning musical

00:01:37.077 --> 00:01:39.500
"In the Heights" to the big screen.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.530
For the song "When the Sun Goes Down,"

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.240
Nina and Benny sing and dance
on the side of a building

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.520
in Washington Heights,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.520
but neither of the actors
wore safety harnesses.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.810
That's because the crew
built a giant replica

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.210
of a brick wall, fire escapes included,

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.130
and rotated it 90 degrees

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:58.470
to give the illusion of the
gravity-defying romance.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.560
The scene was filmed in
front of a green screen,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.090
and the George Washington
Bridge and NYC sky

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.747
were added afterward.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.460
In Disney's live-action "Cruella,"

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.700
costume designer Jenny Beavan was tasked

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.070
with creating 47 different,
yet dramatic, looks

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.027
for Emma Stone's lead character.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:21.330
Like this dress, worn when
Cruella makes a surprise entrance

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:22.700
from dumped rubbish.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.600
As she hops on the back
of the garbage truck,

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.990
the dress reveals a 40-foot train

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:31.230
made from the baroness'
discarded collection of gowns.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.900
The train was so long
and difficult to move in,

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.540
it couldn't actually be worn onto set.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.030
So production had to attach it

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:39.900
when the cameras cut away from Cruella,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.750
but right before the
final exit and reveal.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.160
This dress that accompanied
this military jacket

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.540
had to be enormous enough
to trap the baroness' car

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.000
but light enough for Stone to wear.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.380
Chief pattern cutter Kirsten
Fletcher and her team

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.320
had to cut out 5,060 petals,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.513
which were later stitched on by hand.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.180
And what about this climactic moment

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.580
when the baroness pushes
Cruella off the balcony,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.470
but her skirt transforms
into a life-saving parachute?

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.040
They tried their hand practically,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.090
but ultimately the parachute dress

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.507
had to be designed using CGI.

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:21.973
Ready?

00:03:21.973 --> 00:03:22.856
Yeah!

00:03:22.856 --> 00:03:25.430
Narrator: Almost 30 years
after the original "Space Jam,"

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:28.033
the long-awaited sequel finally arrived.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.640
Hundreds of artists
collaborated to put a new stamp

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.740
on the live-action/animated classic

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:38.040
and bring the 2D Looney
Tune squad into a 3D world.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.470
Real-life basketball stars

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:42.960
were also converted to
2D and 3D characters.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.690
With the many ambitious
layers and textures,

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:47.630
plus a hand-drawn approach,

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:51.570
creating just one of those
hybrid scenes took nearly a year.

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:53.170
They also used motion capture

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:56.145
to get the animated
characters to play basketball.

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.770
For the virtual basketball court showdown,

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:01.860
a massive mo-cap stage was built

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.910
measuring 120,000 cubic feet,

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:08.243
utilizing a setup with over 100 cameras.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.610
The beloved pup from the
children's book and cartoon

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.070
Clifford the Big Red Dog

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:20.850
came to life in the 2021
live-action adaptation.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.920
This required a big red puppet.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.480
Puppeteers Rowan Magee
and Jon Riddleberger

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.440
took on the physically demanding task

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.730
of carrying Clifford for
eight- to 14-hour days,

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:33.940
taking a break every 70 minutes.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:34.822
Woman: Is that enough of the dog?

00:04:34.822 --> 00:04:35.655
Crew: Yeah.

00:04:35.655 --> 00:04:37.613
Woman: And dog exit frame.

00:04:37.613 --> 00:04:38.740
Narrator: So in some scenes,

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:41.232
the puppeteers used gimbals instead.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:42.973
Like in this scene,

00:04:42.973 --> 00:04:45.370
when Clifford's young
owner gets on for a ride.

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:47.020
She was really climbing a ladder

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:49.440
to sit on a gimbal with
springs and wheels.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.010
Hidden crew members pulled the gimbal

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.590
to move as the dog would,

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.798
and a hidden cable attached
to the actor kept her safe.

00:05:00.508 --> 00:05:03.145
All right, here we go, guys. Be careful!

00:05:03.145 --> 00:05:04.503
And action!

00:05:04.503 --> 00:05:06.393
Narrator: M. Night
Shyamalan needed the beach

00:05:06.393 --> 00:05:08.120
to be the villain in the movie "Old,"

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.350
so the team had to make Playa El Valle

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.760
in the Dominican Republic
intense and ominous.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.280
That's where these fake mountains come in.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.390
Production designer
Naaman Marshall and crew

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:19.760
actually built these mountains

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.000
to create a sinister presence.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.920
After taking photographs and measurements

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:25.700
of the site in El Valle,

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.330
Marshall returned to California

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.420
to build their model of the design.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.600
Since the beach already had a rock wall,

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.337
Marshall blended theirs
to it by using plaster,

00:05:34.337 --> 00:05:37.112
scaffolding, foam, paint, and sand.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:43.990
At one point, a hurricane even
destroyed the unfinished set.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:45.740
But in the end, they managed to complete

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:49.240
the 32-foot-tall, 900-foot-wide wall.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.200
To blend it even more seamlessly,

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.230
the visual effects team used
photographs of the natural rock

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.247
to recreate various
textures on the surface.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.720
Actor Bob Odenkirk
reinvented himself physically

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.370
for his role in the action film "Nobody."

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:10.840
Odenkirk is known for playing

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:12.710
one of TV's most famous lawyers

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:15.010
in "Breaking Bad" and "Better Call Saul,"

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:18.060
very much a non-action role.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.590
Typically, actors train
for four to six weeks

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:22.130
for action films.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:23.690
But the 59-year-old comedian

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:26.010
trained in martial arts for two years

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:28.470
with "John Wick" and "Atomic
Blonde" stunt performer

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:30.220
Daniel Bernhardt.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.720
You can see Bernhardt in this scene

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:33.670
playing one of his attackers.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:35.430
Even while filming "Better Call Saul,"

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.470
Odenkirk continued to perfect his skills

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:40.690
in an Albuquerque mixed martial arts gym.

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:42.280
And the first glimpse of this payoff

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.940
happens in this fight scene,
which happens on a real bus.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:47.520
The bus was made stunt-friendly

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:50.510
with hidden padding on the
floor and in the seats.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.820
And Odenkirk did most of the all-out brawl

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:54.550
without a stunt double.

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:56.710
In the end, this bloody six-minute scene

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:58.727
took three nights to film.

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:02.090
In "Reminiscence,"

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.330
Hugh Jackman plays a private investigator

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:07.503
who uses a machine to navigate
his client's memories.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:09.490
But this water tank

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:11.490
and these memory
projections in the machine

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.550
were actually created
without a green screen.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.660
Visual effects supervisor Bruce Jones

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:18.200
worked with director Lisa Joy

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:21.280
to devise a way for the actors
to interact with the memory,

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:22.820
seamlessly walking around

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.430
the moving pieces of footage on the set.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:27.110
The memories were filmed ahead of time,

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:30.620
then projected onto a very
thinly meshed Halo Gauze screen

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:32.350
stretched into a cylinder.

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:34.050
Three 20K projectors were needed

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:37.210
to map the moving images
onto the circular screen.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:39.890
And the mo-cap cameras moved
in sync with the actors

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:43.465
around the screens, keeping
the illusion of a hologram.

00:07:46.737 --> 00:07:48.910
"No Time to Die" had one of the longest

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:50.870
pre-credit James Bond openings,

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.290
clocking in at over 20 minutes.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:57.550
And with lots of in-camera
effects, it was no easy feat.

00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:00.600
The action sequence was
shot on location in Matera

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:03.220
and used 10 Aston Martins.

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.360
For this scene, the crew planted squibs

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:06.910
to replicate bullet impacts

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:09.720
timed perfectly with the car's movements.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.400
Approximately 200 pellets
were fired at the car windows,

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:15.960
which were layered with a
special polycarbonate plastic

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.380
so they wouldn't shatter.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:18.650
And this jump ...

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:21.210
was no green screen.

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:24.070
A stunt driver went
exactly 60 miles per hour

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:27.420
up a 25-foot ramp and
jumped 12 feet in the air

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:29.330
to make the impressive landing.

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:32.097
It took four takes to
get the shot they needed.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:37.500
For "The French Dispatch,"

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.230
Wes Anderson and the production team

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:42.810
created the fictional French
town of Ennui-sur-Blasé

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:45.950
by adding their own touch to
the real city of Angoulême,

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:48.223
located in the southwest of France.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.390
As Anderson frequently does in his films,

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.550
the crew relied on handcrafted sets

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.167
and practical elements shot in-camera.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:00.141
They constructed foreground buildings

00:09:00.141 --> 00:09:03.190
and scaffolding that could
twist and turn for the camera,

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:06.370
added original banners
and signs to store walls,

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:08.910
and built café fronts and trees.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:10.660
Even freeze-frames were recreated

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:14.252
by having actors keep still
while objects hung in midair.

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:18.560
But not all the imaginative moments

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:20.777
were captured completely in-camera.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.470
In the film's prison riot sequence,

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:30.040
Benicio Del Toro's
wheelchair shatters midair.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:31.170
To achieve this effect,

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:34.250
green-suit performers held
each part separately in the air

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:36.592
to precisely manipulate the wheels.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:42.080
Mirror shots are notoriously
difficult to film

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.647
without the camera being seen.

00:09:44.647 --> 00:09:46.830
"Last Night in Soho"
had the added challenge

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:49.570
of having one character,
Eloise, on one side

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:52.670
and a different character,
Sandie, on the other.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:55.340
The production design team
built a double lobby set

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:58.870
so Sandie and Eloise could
follow each other in the mirror.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:01.350
It got more complicated when
other actors were in frame,

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.280
like in this shot, when Jack joins Eloise

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:04.520
in front of the mirror.

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.120
That is actor Matt
Smith's actual reflection

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:09.930
in a real mirror, but
it's been cut in half.

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:12.700
On the other side is Sandie
in front of a green screen;

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:15.750
VFX later adjusted her
background to match.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:19.360
Perhaps the most physically taxing trick

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:22.110
comes during this elaborate
mirrored dance sequence.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.090
The two actors had to prep for weeks

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:25.580
and work with a movement coach

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:27.930
to make sure not a single eyeline was off,

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:30.230
nor body movement unmatched.

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:32.610
Then, to capture it all,
a Steadicam operator

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:35.670
referred to on set as
"the human motion control"

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:39.545
had to shoot every mirrored
shot the exact same way.

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:44.860
In "Army of the Dead,"

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:49.260
the zombie tiger Valentine
may have stolen the show.

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:52.960
The VFX team based Valentine
on a white tiger in Florida,

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:55.970
which they later found out
was owned by Carole Baskin

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:59.110
of Netflix's "Tiger King" documentary.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:01.050
The team recorded and studied the animal

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:03.570
to nail down the proportions,
movement, and light

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.170
for the zombie version.

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:07.210
Later, they created the emaciated look

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:09.820
with old dried bloodstains
and a missing eye

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:13.250
to blend it in with the
postapocalyptic environment.

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:15.830
A stuntperson in a green
suit stood in for eyelines

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:17.810
and in the interactive scenes.

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:19.900
And for the scene of the
tiger mauling Martin,

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.680
cables were used to throw him around

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.455
as if the tiger was
swinging him with his jaws.

00:11:29.429 --> 00:11:30.554
Director: Three,

00:11:30.554 --> 00:11:31.560
two,

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:32.570
one,

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:33.403
action!

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:36.980
Narrator: The MCU
blockbuster "Black Widow"

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:39.943
was filmed in multiple
cities across the globe.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:43.000
But the most visually stunning chase scene

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:44.790
was captured in Budapest.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:46.940
Production filmed for
two weeks in the city,

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:48.900
with motorcycles and armored vehicles

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:51.070
speeding wildly through city streets.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:52.500
Special effects teams built

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.560
some of the large Taskmaster
tanks you see here.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.790
And a total of four BMWs were
destroyed in the process,

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:02.632
with one exploding in the air
on a real Budapest street.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:08.010
The opening scene of
"A Quiet Place Part II"

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:10.533
raised the stakes of its
predecessor by showing

00:12:10.533 --> 00:12:13.392
the characters' first
encounter with the monsters.

00:12:13.392 --> 00:12:16.490
The chaotic sequence was
done with very few cuts.

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:18.290
This was especially tricky for the moment

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:20.130
when Emily Blunt's character, Evelyn,

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:22.060
has to quickly reverse her car

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:23.870
as a mangled bus heads towards them

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:25.737
at over 40 miles per hour.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:29.290
A series of cameras were mounted

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:33.040
inside and out of Evelyn's
car with a custom Biscuit rig.

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:33.930
At the top of the car,

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:35.850
a stunt driver operated the vehicle

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.283
as Blunt pretended to be in control.

00:12:42.082 --> 00:12:42.915
Director: Action!

00:12:42.915 --> 00:12:44.306
Katy: Hold on, everybody!

00:12:44.306 --> 00:12:45.770
Narrator: The bus sequence in "Shang-Chi"

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:48.850
raised the stakes higher than
many Marvel fights before it

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:51.593
by setting the action on a moving surface.

00:12:52.580 --> 00:12:56.460
It took a month to film this
nearly seven-minute sequence.

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:58.640
The actors all went
through intense training

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:00.750
for the film's hand-to-hand combat,

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:04.350
and Simu Liu did a good portion
of the stunts by himself.

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:06.320
For the scenes that show
the outside of the bus,

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:07.950
several real buses were filmed

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:10.020
driving through San Francisco.

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:11.640
Two separate buses were shipped

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:13.720
from California to Sydney, Australia,

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:17.230
to be deconstructed and used
in a blue-screen studio.

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:20.280
Both were suspended:
one on a 15-foot-high,

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:23.640
six-axis gimbal for the
leaps, twisting, and turning,

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.430
and the other slightly off the ground

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:28.830
to simulate the bus shaking
and general driving shots.

00:13:32.617 --> 00:13:35.410
"Ghostbusters: Afterlife"
stayed true to the original

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:37.480
by bringing back the terror dog

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:40.438
and the original's cutting-edge
creature effects work.

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:46.740
"Afterlife"'s crew decided to
use as little CGI as possible,

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.810
building their monsters from scratch.

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:53.250
The practical terror dog
ultimately weighed 450 pounds,

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:55.900
thanks to its mechanized
motors called servos,

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:57.793
which allowed specific movements.

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:01.480
The monster's face alone had 21 servos

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:03.050
controlled by puppeteers

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:05.933
to create detailed movements
like blinking and sniffing.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:09.040
These servos were crucial
to making the character

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:10.873
look as real as possible.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:13.700
But traditional cables also helped

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:15.730
create convincing movements.

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:17.257
The dog's leg in this scene

00:14:17.257 --> 00:14:20.570
used just an isolated
leg controlled by cables.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:22.640
The puppeteer could
wear the leg on his arm,

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:24.233
manipulating its movement.

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:26.820
And because the leg was so big,

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:28.700
cables helped move its ankles and toes

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:31.266
where the puppeteer's
real arm couldn't reach.

